    "long_instructions": "**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Fit the model by calling `model.fit` with `train_inputs` and `train_outputs`.\r\n- Measure your model’s loss. First, call `model.predict` with `test_inputs`. Then, calculate the mean squared error of those predictions against the `test_outputs`.\r\n- Print your model’s loss. \r\n\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nRemember you can fit a model with:\r\n\r\n```\r\nmodel.fit(train_inputs, train_outputs)\r\n```\r\n\r\nTo measure your model’s loss on the test data, first use the fitted model to make predictions from the test inputs.\r\n\r\n```\r\npredictions = model.predict(test_inputs)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen, compute the mean squared error by comparing those predictions with the actual test outputs.\r\n\r\n```\r\nmse = mean_squared_error(test_outputs, predictions)\r\n```\r\n\r\nConsider rounding the result as you print it.\r\n\r\n```\r\nprint(round(mse, 2))\r\n```",
